# Generated by Cargo
# will have compiled files and executables
debug/
target/

# Remove Cargo.lock from gitignore if creating an executable, leave it for libraries
# More information here https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/guide/cargo-toml-vs-cargo-lock.html

# These are backup files generated by rustfmt
**/*.rs.bk

# MSVC Windows builds of rustc generate these, which store debugging information
*.pdb


# Added by cargo
.vscode
node_modules
dist
dhat*.json
default_*
report.*
.shuttle*
Secrets*.toml
Cargo.lock
.claude/settings.local.json

# VitePress docs site
website/node_modules/
website/.vitepress/dist/
website/.vitepress/cache/
# Generated markdown (from scripts/build-website.sh)
website/index.md
website/plugins/
website/crates/
website/guide/
# Legacy docsify leftovers
website/en/
website/assets/docsify-app.js
website/assets/style.css
website/README.md
website/_sidebar.md
website/_navbar.md
.gstack/

# tsc -b incremental caches, rewritten by every web build
web/*.tsbuildinfo

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# pingap-waf fork additions. Everything above this line is vendored from
# upstream pingap and is left byte-identical so `git merge upstream/main`
# never conflicts on it. Add fork-specific rules below only.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Cargo.lock is tracked here. Upstream ignores it (it publishes as a library)
# yet force-tracks it anyway; this fork is a deployed application and a WAF, so
# a reproducible dependency graph is a security property, and CI asserts
# against Cargo.lock directly (exactly one pingora version, no 0.6.0).
!Cargo.lock

# dist/README.md is tracked via `git add -f`, exactly as upstream tracks it.
# A negation cannot work here: line 19's bare `dist` excludes the directory, and
# git will not re-include a file whose parent directory is excluded. The
# placeholder has to be tracked so a fresh clone compiles at all — rust-embed's
# `#[folder = "dist/"]` in src/plugin/admin.rs requires the directory to exist.
# Being already tracked, it stays tracked; the ignore rule no longer applies.

# Phase 02 spikes are throwaway and excluded from the workspace; their build
# output must never reach history.
spikes/*/target/

# Developer-local working trees, never published. `.xia-src` holds third-party
# reference clones, each carrying its own .git — tracking them would create
# gitlinks to repositories nobody else can resolve. `plans` holds the local
# plan/report tree. `CLAUDE.md` holds agent instructions for working in this
# checkout. None of the three is part of the product.
/.xia-src/
/plans/
/CLAUDE.md

# Control-plane store (Phase 07) and any local SQLite artifact
*.db
*.db-shm
*.db-wal

# Key material and secrets never enter this repo. Blanket-matching is the right
# default for a security product, so the two legitimate exceptions are named
# explicitly rather than by loosening the rule.
*.pem
*.key
.env
.env.*

# Let's Encrypt's public intermediate CAs (E5/E6/R10/R11), vendored by upstream
# and `include_bytes!`d at compile time in pingap-certificate/src/chain.rs:20-23.
# Public certificates, not key material — and because the inclusion is
# compile-time, ignoring them turns `cargo build` into a hard error.
!pingap-certificate/assets/*.pem

# Local tooling state
.code-review-graph/

# libFuzzer's evolved corpus is a coverage cache, not a gate: it is machine
# generated, it grows on every run, and nothing asserts against its contents.
# The Phase 03 gate is "both targets run clean with zero artifacts", and the
# Phase 02 detector corpus — which *is* a gate — is hand-assembled and hashed.
# `artifacts/` is deliberately NOT ignored: a crash reproducer is the regression
# fixture for the bug it found and belongs in history.
crates/pingap-waf/fuzz/corpus/
